\section{Elements}{
Every fit has these elements:
\itemize{
\item \code{scale_est}: data frame with one row per optimized covariate (row names
are covariate names) and two columns. \code{Mean} is the estimated sigma in
map units, and \code{SE} is its Hessian-based standard error. \code{SE} is \code{NA} for
hard-radius landscape and unweighted surface metrics and for every radius
estimated under \code{kernel = "fixed"}, because their likelihood changes in
steps.
\item \code{shape_est}: data frame with the same layout for the shape parameter
when \code{kernel = "expow"}, otherwise \code{NULL}. Hard-radius covariates have
\code{NA} shape and shape SE because a shape does not apply to them; their
unused shape positions are left out of the likelihood parameter count.
\item \code{optim_results}: the raw list returned by \code{\link[stats:optim]{stats::optim()}} or
\code{\link[optimParallel:optimParallel]{optimParallel::optimParallel()}}, including \code{par}, \code{value} (the negative
log-likelihood), \code{hessian}, and convergence codes. It also holds
\code{par_unscale} (sigma values in map units) and \code{hessian_unscale}. For a
radius grid, this list describes the selected combination: its Hessian
covers only the smooth parameters at those fixed radii, with \code{NA} for
hard-radius rows and columns and any inactive shape. See
\code{diagnostics(fit)$radius_profile} for every candidate.
\item \code{opt_mod}: your model refit at the estimated scales. Use it for
coefficients, inference, prediction, and \code{\link[=plot_marginal_effects]{plot_marginal_effects()}}. Its
coefficients are on the model's link scale (for example log for a Poisson
model), and by default each optimized covariate is standardized, so a
coefficient describes the change in the linear predictor for one standard
deviation of the covariate.
\item \code{fitted_mod_original}: the \code{fitted_mod} you supplied, kept for
reference.
\item \code{min_D}, \code{max_D}: the lower and upper bounds for sigma during the
search, in map units.
\item \code{kernel_inputs}: the \code{kernel_inputs} list (see \link[=multiScaleR_data-class]{multiScaleR_data}),
without \code{min_D} and \code{max_D}, which are stored separately.
\item \code{scl_params}: named list with \code{mean} and \code{sd} vectors, the centering and
scaling values of each optimized covariate at the estimated scales.
\code{\link[=kernel_scale.raster]{kernel_scale.raster()}} uses them when \code{scale_center = TRUE} so that maps
match the model's covariates. With \code{covariate_transform = "raw"}, the
centers are zero and the scales one; prediction never relearns these
values from new data.
\item \code{join_by}: the \code{join_by} data frame, or \code{NULL}.
\item \code{opt_context}: internal record of the model-class-specific refit logic,
kept for \code{\link[=profile_sigma]{profile_sigma()}}. With \code{covariate_transform = "raw"} it also
records that setting. \code{NULL} for the analytic backends.
\item \code{profile_scale_est}: data frame of profile-likelihood intervals, or
\code{NULL} until \code{\link[=profile_sigma]{profile_sigma()}} has been run.
\item \code{diagnostics}: list of diagnostic records; read it with \code{\link[=diagnostics]{diagnostics()}}.
\item \code{next_run}: list of suggested settings for a follow-up fit: \code{max_D} for
the next \code{\link[=kernel_prep]{kernel_prep()}} call (raised when an estimate approaches the
current \code{max_D}), the estimated \code{start_sigma} values in map units,
\code{start_shape} for \code{kernel = "expow"}, and \code{start_par}, the starts on the
internal scale that \code{multiScale_optim()} expects, scaled to that \code{max_D}.
\code{n_cores} is \code{NULL}; ask \code{\link[=estimate_multiscale_ram]{estimate_multiscale_ram()}} for a parallel
worker count. \code{NULL} for some analytic backends.
\item \code{warn_message}: integer vector of warning codes. It starts with \code{0} and
adds \code{1} when an estimate approaches \code{max_D}, \code{2} when a sigma standard
