\section{Elements added by the analytic backends}{
\itemize{
\item \code{profile_fit}, \code{uncertainty}, \code{dispersion_est}: present for
\code{"profile_glm"} and \code{"joint_nb2"}. They keep the transformed parameter
estimates, the starts and their convergence scores, the profile and joint
information matrices, identifiability diagnostics, and NB2 theta.
\code{uncertainty$available} says whether joint uncertainty passed its checks,
and \code{uncertainty$reasons} says why not.
\code{uncertainty$joint_available} is also \code{TRUE} when joint uncertainty holds
bounded kernel parameters fixed, and \code{uncertainty$joint_conditional_on}
names them. \code{opt_mod} and its coefficient
summary condition on the estimated kernel parameters. Use
\code{\link[=vcov.multiScaleR]{vcov.multiScaleR()}} and \code{\link[=predict.multiScaleR]{predict.multiScaleR()}} for joint uncertainty.
\item \code{profile_fit} and \code{uncertainty} for \code{"conditional_logit"}, which keep
the starts, every start outcome, and the selected start. Scale
uncertainty is not available.
\item \code{mixed_fit} for \code{"joint_mixed"}: the kernel engine, log-scale estimates
and bounds in internal distance units, the independent starts, every start
outcome and warning, the selected start, and the variables that
prediction and profile refits need. \code{scale_est$SE} and the log-scale
Wald limits in \code{profile_scale_est} come from the selected start's joint Hessian, one
scale at a time. \code{uncertainty$available} is \code{FALSE} because coefficient
and prediction uncertainty still condition on the scales. \code{next_run} and
the \code{max_D} warning follow the analytic GLM backends, and \code{opt_context} is
\code{NULL}.
}

\link{multiScaleR-fitting-methods} explains what each backend records and why
uncertainty may be withheld.
}

\section{Where each quantity lives}{
\tabular{lll}{
   Quantity \tab Where to find it \tab Scale \cr
   Scale of effect (sigma) \tab \code{fit$scale_est$Mean}, \code{summary(fit)} \tab Map units \cr
   Kernel shape \tab \code{fit$shape_est$Mean} \tab Shape units of the exponential power kernel \cr
   Effective distance \tab \code{summary(fit)}, \code{\link[=kernel_dist]{kernel_dist()}} \tab Map units \cr
   Regression coefficients \tab \code{coef(fit$opt_mod)}, \code{summary(fit)} \tab Link scale, per standard deviation of each covariate by default \cr
